    {
      "ESSeqNum": 0,
      "FacilityID": 1000106882,
      "SummaryText": "Executive Summary\n\nBorregaard USA, Inc., located in Rothschild, Wisconsin, manufactures lignosulfonate specialty chemicals using spent sulfite liquors and other regulated and non-regulated substances. A formaldehyde solution (37%) is used as a raw material and is covered under the EPA Risk Management Program.\n\nBorregaard operates under a comprehensive accident prevention program that includes process hazard analysis, operating procedures, maintenance, training, and management of change systems. A detailed review of the formaldehyde process has been completed, and all identified hazards have been corrected or controlled. The facility has operated for over seventy years with a strong safety record and no reportable accidental releases.\n\nWorst-Case Release Scenario\n\nThe worst-case scenario is a catastrophic rupture of the formaldehyde storage tank containing a maximum of 8,550 gallons of 37% formaldehyde solution at 93 \u00b0F. which is 28,243 pounds of formaldehyde. The tank is located inside a building with containment dike.  The entire tank and containment dike are in a secure area that is a part of a larger containment area that is contained to a wastewater treatment plant.  \n\nAlternative Release Scenario\n\nA credible alternative release involves a 3-inch transfer hose rupture during unloading at 85 pounds of formaldehyde solution with a truck emergency shutoff activating within 10 seconds, which remains fully contained within the mitigation system. The endpoint distance is 0.3 mile, contained within the industrial property boundary.  In the outside unloading area, there is a trench drain leading to the 1,090-gallon sump that directs material to the wastewater system and would capture the formaldehyde solution from the hose rupture.  \n\nEmergency Response Program\n\nBorregaard maintains a written Emergency Response Program in accordance with EPCRA and 40 CFR Part 68, including procedures for spill control, notification, coordination with emergency responders, and employee t"
    },
    {
      "ESSeqNum": 1,
      "FacilityID": 1000106882,
      "SummaryText": "raining. Copies have been provided to the Marathon County LEPC and the Rothschild Fire Department.\n\nBorregaard is committed to safe operation, environmental protection, and transparent communication with employees, regulators, and the community."
    }
  ]
